@dblecture{Frenk.2011,
 abstract = {Vor Wort:
The representations of space in the Bond film franchise serve a larger purpose than simply providing a backdrop for the action and the characters. As already in Fleming's novels, space in the films is often spectacular and extreme. lt is highly recognisable yet excitingly out of the ordinary, all the while remaining - as some would say - utterly predictable. Its obvious narrative and ideological functions make space integral to the workings and meanings of the films, for instance providing the audience with special visual pleasures and terrors or with ideological messages about the benefits of 'civilisation' and certain ways of ordering the world. 007 has been called a defender of empire, the world's policeman, a super tourist, and a pioneer and symbol of glo- balisation (Black 3; Baron 144-5; Pankratz 133; Tesche v); he is continually crossing, (re)conquering, and consuming all sorts of space.},
